WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.870
OLIVIER ASSAYAS:<i> I think we are
 in a moment of transformation.</i>

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:08.441
<i> People are watching movies
 on different devices.</i>

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:11.543
<i> But ultimately, it's all about
 the freedom of creation.</i>

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:15.015 line:0%
And the fact that people
will be enjoying it
in different formats.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:20.920
<i> I made the movie,</i>
Irma Vep,<i> in 1996,</i>

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:23.589
and I thought it was
an excellent starting point.

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:25.791
ALICIA VIKANDER:
<i> The series doesn't feel like</i>

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:27.260
<i> it's a remake for him.</i>

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:29.896 line:0%
He loves to call it
a continuation of his film.

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:35.301
OLIVIER: Les Vampires
<i>was one of the best serials ever
 by Louis Feuillade,</i>

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.270
<i> who was one of the pioneers
 of French cinema.</i>

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.373
And at the center of this
is Musidora,

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:42.541
who was the original actress.

00:00:43.475 --> 00:00:47.213
It is quite extraordinary
to go back to 1914

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:50.617
and see this character
in her silk catsuit,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.986
and you realize all these
famous action characters,

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:55.421
or female villains.

00:00:55.454 --> 00:00:58.157
It suddenly seems like
it was all inspired

00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:00.260
<i> from this one character.</i>

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:03.830
The ghost of Irma Vep
has been haunting cinema
ever since.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.966
OLIVIER:<i> My first intuition
 was to go to someone</i>

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:09.401
who I admire very much,
with Nicolas Ghesquière,

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:11.337
<i>the designer for Louis Vuitton.</i>

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:16.075
<i> And I wanted him to reinvent
 the catsuit for our time.</i>

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:20.279 line:0%
At the beginning, we spoke with
Nicolas about the materials

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:23.850 line:0%
because they had to be
sensual and enigmatic

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:26.819
<i> and not too difficult
 for her to play with.</i>

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:29.989
I knew that I would have to
create this character

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:32.759
from a very physical point
to stand out

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.594
<i> with this kind of costume.</i>

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.497 line:0%
I did some music
for the Vampire Ballet,

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.000
<i> and I worked really closely
 with the choreographer.</i>

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.036
<i> He would choregraph to
these rhythms I was sending him.</i>

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.473 line:0%
I was really honored to work
with Angelin Preljocaj,

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:50.509
<i> the choreographer, and I had
 the chance to rehearse it
 with him,</i>

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:51.444
and to do the scene

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:53.780
because he plays
his own character on the film.

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:57.449
DENIS LENOIR:<i> One of
 the blessing of this project</i>

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:59.785
<i> is the locations
 are so gorgeous</i>

00:01:59.818 --> 00:02:01.687 line:0%
that it's going to be excellent.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:03.455
When you have
the right location,

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.124
<i> the set becomes alive.</i>

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:07.793
<i>And all of a sudden, the scene,
 it becomes stronger.</i>

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:12.498 line:0%
We did a casting of rooftops
in Paris, it was invaluable.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.736
VINCENT MACAIGNE:
<i> Olivier captured it
 in a magic way.</i>

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.738 line:0%
In a way, I rediscovered Paris.

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:22.241 line:0%
The production design
really helped everyone

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:25.177 line:0%
to be immersed in
our environment.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:28.047 line:0%
The main thing for me
was to invent something

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.650 line:0%
based on Feuillade,
but more modern.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:32.418
<i> Maybe more fun.</i>

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:36.089 line:0%
It enriches the sets
in such a way

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.057
<i> that I feel it's not a set.</i>

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:40.927
<i> I feel like this is just
 a part of the world.</i>

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.863
That's the magic of
the worlds you create,

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:45.097
and you step into.

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.368
Sets are a limitation
of reality.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:51.103
(KNOCKS ON WALL)
Just like dreams.

00:02:52.571 --> 00:02:54.607
OLIVIER:<i> Whatever I write,
 to me, is the starting point.</i>

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:57.543
<i> I don't want to hear the pages
 of my screenplay turning,</i>

00:02:57.576 --> 00:03:01.714
I want to be the first viewer
of something we are
inventing together.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:04.784
♪ (DRAMATIC VIOLIN
MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

